    core: `Your Moon meets your Mercury at the same degree. What you feel and how you think aren't separate systems. They run on the same current. Your emotional body and your cognitive function are fused. What you feel, you can name. How you articulate, you can feel in your body. Your mind isn't analyzing your feelings from outside. It's inside the feeling, thinking from within emotional experience rather than about it. This produces unusual fluency in emotional articulation that others read as emotionally intelligent, present, and unusually able to put feelings into words.`,
    behavioral: `Because your emotional function and your cognitive function are fused, your psychological processing runs through both channels simultaneously. Thinking is emotional work. Feeling is mental work. You can't separate the two. Talking through a feeling and having the feeling are the same process. Writing about emotion and processing emotion are identical activities. The fusion means your thinking is never purely abstract. It's always colored by what your body is feeling. And your feelings are never purely somatic. They arrive with words already attached. The development task is recognizing that not everyone processes emotion verbally, and that silence about feelings doesn't mean absence of feelings.`,
    friendship: `Friends experience you as someone who can name what's happening emotionally in the room before others have registered it. You put feelings into words naturally. The friendship benefits from your emotional articulacy.`,
    romantic: `Partners feel met when you talk about feelings. Verbal exchange about emotion is intimacy for you. You process the relationship by talking about it. Silence about feelings can register as emotional absence even when feeling is present.`,
    feed: `Your Moon and Mercury meet at the same degree in your natal chart. What you feel and how you think run on the same frequency. Emotional processing is verbal for you.`,
